.TH VDR-SUITE.CONF 5 "Phase 65" "vdr-suite" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
vdr-suite.conf \- configuration file for vdr-suite
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vdr-suite.conf
is the planned configuration file for vdr-suite.

It will describe database, HTTP listener and VDR backend settings used by the daemon.
Until the file format is finalized, selected runtime settings are available as environment variables.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VDR_SUITE_DATABASE_PATH
Overrides the SQLite database path.
.TP
.B VDR_SUITE_RECORDING_ARTWORK_ROOTS
Semicolon-separated mapping of backend IDs to absolute local Recording artwork roots.
Each entry uses the form
.IR backend=/absolute/path .
For example:
.PP
.nf
VDR_SUITE_RECORDING_ARTWORK_ROOTS=default=/var/cache/vdr/plugins/tvscraper;wohnhaus2=/srv/vdr-artwork
.fi
.PP
There is no universal default. TVScraper accepts
.BR -d / --dir
and otherwise derives its storage directory from the VDR cache directory, which is distribution and installation dependent.
The configured root must therefore match the actual local TVScraper directory for that backend.

Duplicate backend IDs, relative paths and malformed entries disable local artwork delivery for the parsed setting.
A backend without an explicit mapping cannot use another backend's root.

Recording artwork references received from a backend remain internal.
The daemon resolves them only below the root assigned to that backend and exposes opaque, authenticated
.B /recording-artwork/
URLs to clients.
Missing roots, unsupported file types, invalid image signatures, oversized files and paths escaping an allowed root are rejected.
.TP
.B VDR_SUITE_MEDIA_X264_PRESET
Server-side software H.264 transcode preset policy.
Accepted values are
.BR auto ,
.BR superfast ,
.BR veryfast ,
.B faster
and
.BR fast .
The default is
.BR auto .
Auto uses calibrated workload measurements when available and otherwise uses conservative typed defaults.
.TP
.B VDR_SUITE_MEDIA_X264_STANDARD_PRESET
Optional override for ordinary HD-or-lower software video transcodes.
Accepted values are the same as for
.BR VDR_SUITE_MEDIA_X264_PRESET .
.TP
.B VDR_SUITE_MEDIA_X264_DEINTERLACE_PRESET
Optional override for interlaced-to-progressive software video transcodes.
Accepted values are the same as for
.BR VDR_SUITE_MEDIA_X264_PRESET .
.TP
.B VDR_SUITE_MEDIA_X264_UHD_PRESET
Optional override for software video transcodes whose source is larger than 1920x1080.
Accepted values are the same as for
.BR VDR_SUITE_MEDIA_X264_PRESET .
.TP
.B VDR_SUITE_MEDIA_TRANSCODE_PROFILE
Overrides the measured media-transcode performance profile path.
The default is
.IR /var/lib/vdr-suite/media-transcode-performance.conf .
The profile is normally generated by
.BR vdr-suite-media-calibrate .
The daemon never benchmarks during playback or startup.
A media-session encoder decision is made before the worker starts and remains stable for the session lifetime.
.SH MEDIA TRANSCODE CALIBRATION
The optional operator command
.PP
.nf
sudo vdr-suite-media-calibrate
sudo systemctl restart vdr-suite-daemon
.fi
.PP
measures representative software-x264 workloads and stores per-workload preset throughput.
Generated fixtures use a short benchmark duration.
Explicitly supplied real Recording references use a sustained 30-second sample by default and include source decode plus the normal AAC audio fallback when present.
The real-reference duration can be adjusted with
.BR --real-seconds .
The accepted profile format is version 3. Older profile versions are ignored and fall back to conservative workload defaults.
In auto mode VDR-Suite selects the slowest/highest-compression supported preset that has at least 1.25x measured real-time throughput.
If no measured preset reaches the threshold, the typed fallback remains
.B superfast
for deinterlacing and
.B veryfast
for the standard and UHD-source software paths.
.SH LOCATION
The planned system configuration path is:
.PP
.I /etc/vdr-suite/vdr-suite.conf
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vdr-suite-daemon (8),
.BR vdr-suite-dashboard (1)
.SH STATUS
This manual page documents the current environment boundary while the final configuration file format remains under development.
